1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:13,099
This is the blacksmith. It runs the analysis: it loads your data, computes what you ask for, trains and tests models, and builds small apps to explore the results.

2
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:27,600
It loads your data tables, such as CSV and Excel files, computes the features and statistics you ask for, and trains and evaluates classifiers. It builds interactive apps so you can explore a dataset.

3
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:39,700
What makes it different: it runs the computation itself on your data, and leaves the commented code beside the result, so anyone can repeat it.

4
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:51,000
The blacksmith covers the computing. The artist turns its numbers into publication figures, the bookworm compares them with the literature, and the adversary checks the method.

5
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:00,300
It reads your original data and leaves it untouched. Each new result goes in a new, numbered file.

6
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:13,600
A real run, on simulated data. Growth medium turned out to be mixed up with country, so the member asks for the serum effect within each country, with a matching test.

7
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:25,700
The answer is Done. The effect holds, but the countries disagree, and the blacksmith says so. It saves the results and the code that made them.

8
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:38,200
Say what you want built: an app to explore your quality control, a classifier tested the way your data demand, or a series of steps on a counts table.

9
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:47,300
It works in Python by default; name a template script in your request and it follows it.

10
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:59,400
To use it, type to Claude Code and name it: ask the blacksmith to compute, train or build something. Tell it which data, and what you want out.

11
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:12,000
The reply starts with Done, Failed or Partial. Then come the numbers, the files and the code, so the adversary can run it again.
